{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1786429211,"nonce":"d/Mv29GSgTknftpdZb9fMlAz1yQ=","message":"PARTIAL — budget ran out, but with two real findings already.\n\nThe load driver in test/load/ was being built against real Postgres with the fake-Slack conformance server (test/harness/slack_conformance.go) counting calls, so 'Slack calls actually made' would be measured rather than estimated.\n\nFindings before it was cut short:\n1. THE SHEDDER FIRES on a 2-CPU/4GB colima VM under a 500-alert batch — so the shed path is not theoretical at modest scale, which is exactly what this issue suspected nobody knew.\n2. The driver's own retry budget was SHORTER than the Retry-After oto asks for, which is itself the interesting shape: a client that gives up before oto's advertised backoff is Alertmanager's five-minute retry budget in miniature (ADR 0007).\n\nStill to do: the sustained several-thousand-over-minutes case, the recorded numbers artefact under test/load/, and the invariant assertions (exactly one storm notice per channel, no duplicate delivery, ordering gate makes progress, rollups O(groups) not O(alerts) end to end — the unit-level version of that last one is already proved in grouping/service/joinmany_test.go).","files":null}]}